# A S‐Scheme MOF‐on‐MOF Heterostructure

## abstract
Constructing MOF‐on‐MOF heterojunction with elaborate charge transfer mechanism and interface is a promising strategy for improving the photocatalytic properties of MOFs. Herein, a Step‐scheme (S‐scheme) MIL‐125‐NH2@CoFe Prussian blue analogue (PBA) heterojunction is reported for the first time. The MOF‐on‐MOF heterostructure exhibits a sandwich‐like morphology with hollow CoFe PBA nanocages selectively assembled on the top‐down surfaces of MIL‐125‐NH2 nanocakes. Experimental findings and theoretical simulation results reveal the formation of internal electric field via interfacial TiOCo bonds at the heterojunction, providing driving force and atomic transportation highway for accelerating the S‐scheme charge transfer and enhancing the redox performance. Contributed further by the hollow sandwich‐like structures with increased active site exposure, the designed MOF‐on‐MOF heterojunction exhibits significantly enhanced photocatalytic activity for degradation of various organic pollutants. This study provides insights toward the rational design of semiconducting MOF‐based heterojunctions with improved properties.

Constructing MOF-on-MOF heterojunction with elaborate charge transfer mechanism and interface is a promising strategy for improving the photocatalytic properties of MOFs. Herein, a Step-scheme (S-scheme) $M\vert L\vert-125.N\vert H_{2}@mathsf C o F$ e Prussian blue analogue (PBA) heterojunction is reported for the first time. The MOF-on-MOF heterostructure exhibits a sandwich-like morphology with hollow CoFe PBA nanocages selectively assembled on the top-down surfaces of MIL-125- $N H_{2}$ nanocakes. Experimental findings and theoretical simulation results reveal the formation of internal electric field via interfacial ${\tt T i-O-C o}$ bonds at the heterojunction, providing driving force and atomic transportation highway for accelerating the S-scheme charge transfer and enhancing the redox performance. Contributed further by the hollow sandwich-like structures with increased active site exposure, the designed MOF-on-MOF heterojunction exhibits significantly enhanced photocatalytic activity for degradation of various organic pollutants. This study provides insights toward the rational design of semiconducting MOF-based heterojunctions with improved properties.
## 1. Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) constructed by coordina­ tion of metal ions/clusters and organic ligands are an appealing class of crystalline porous materials.[1–6] Owing to their crys­ talline framework, large specific surface area, high porosity, tunable compositions, structural para­ meters, and ligand-metal charge transfer pathway, semiconducting MOFs have been widely investigated as promising photocatalysts in pollutant degradation, water splitting, organic transformation, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ fixation.[7–11] It is reported that pristine semiconducting MOFs with simple compositions may suffer from photocarrier recombina­ tion with restricted photo­catalytic prop­ erties.[12,13] To address this challenge, hybridization of semiconducting MOFs and another semiconductor for construc­ tion of heterojunction is one of the most effective and widely studied strategies, among which an innovative Step-scheme (S-scheme) heterojunction has attracted growing interest. Compared to traditional type I (straddling gap) and II (staggered gap) heterojunctions, the internal electric field at the interface of S-scheme hetero­ junction can promote spatial charge separation and enhance the redox ability,[14–20] thus the photocatalytic performance can be improved.
To design MOF-based S-scheme heterojunctions, tremen­ dous efforts have been devoted to the discovery of appropriate semiconductor couples with well-matched band structures and Fermi levels.[14,21] Various non-MOF semiconductors such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ,[22,23] CdS,[24] and $\mathrm{g}–\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}[25]$ have been integrated with MOFs for the preparation of MOF-based S-scheme heterojunc­ tions. Recent advances in MOFs have given rise to a fascinating design of MOF-on-MOF heterostructures by conjugating two or more different MOFs into one hybrid material. It is hypothe­ sized that the construction of MOF-on-MOF heterojunction can integrate the advantages of different semiconducting MOFs, beyond the conventional MOF/non-MOF hybridization at a single MOF level.[26–29] The interfacial interaction of different MOF units with sufficient surface-exposed metals and ligands may offer atomic-level charge transport channels in MOF-onMOF heterojunction, facilitating the directional charge transfer and reinforcing the photocatalytic performances.[30–34] There­ fore, construction of MOF-on-MOF S-scheme heterojunction is a promising strategy for improving the photocatalytic proper­ ties of MOFs, yet rarely reported.
Herein, a novel S-scheme MOF-on-MOF heterojunction with internal electric field and interfacial chemical bonds is con­ structed by coupling $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ with CoFe Prussian blue analogues (PBA) via an interfacial growth-ion exchange route. The MOF-on-MOF heterostructures exhibit a sandwich-like morphology with hollow CoFe PBA nanocages selectively assembled on the top-down surfaces of $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ nano­ cakes. Density functional theory calculation combined with experimental studies reveal the formation of internal electric field and interfacial $_{\mathrm{Ti-O-Co}}$ bonds between MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and CoFe PBA with appropriate band structure and Fermi level. During photocatalysis, the internal electric field provides intense driving force for photoelectron transfer from MIL${125.}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ to CoFe PBA via the $_{\mathrm{Ti-O-Co}}$ bonds at the interface as fast pathways. Together with the enhanced surface acces­ sibility contributed by sandwich-like architecture and hollow structure of CoFe PBA, MIL-125-NH2@CoFe PBA heterostruc­ tures exhibit significantly enhanced photocatalytic activity for tetracycline degradation compared to single $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , CoFe PBA, and physical mixture.
## 2. Results and Discussion
The overall synthetic approach of MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA is illustrated in Figure 1a. First, MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $\mathrm{{MIL}=}$ Material Institute Lavoisier) was fabricated as the starting material.[35]
From the field-emission scanning electron microscope (SEM), transmission electron microscopy (TEM), and X-ray diffrac­ tion (XRD) characterization (Figures S1 and S2, Supporting Information), the prepared $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ showed a cake-like morphology and high crystallinity. The average particle size is estimated to be $\approx0.5–1.5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . Through a site-selective inter­ facial growth process,[26] ZIF-67 $\mathrm{\cdotZIF}\ =$ zeolitic imidazolate frameworks) nanocrystals with an average diameter of ${\approx}200~\mathrm{nm}$ were selectively deposited on the top and down surfaces of MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ cakes (Figure S3, Supporting Information) rather than the side surfaces. The XRD pattern of MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ ZIF-67 shows the co-existence of diffraction peaks of MIL${125.}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and ZIF-67 (Figure S4, Supporting Information). The structural information of sandwich-like MIL-125- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}@$ ZIF-67 is in accordance with the reported results.[26]
Afterward, MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA heterostructures were formed via an ion exchange process in $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{F e}(\mathsf{C N})_{6}]^{3-}$ solution, where the ZIF-67 in MIL-125 ${\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}67$ was selectively con­ verted into another MOF, CoFe PBA. The SEM images of MIL$125\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA (Figure ${\underline{{1\mathrm{b}}}},\mathrm{c}$ ) show that the sandwich-like morphology is well kept. The TEM image (Figure 1d; Figure S5, Supporting Information) displays that the solid ZIF-67 nanocrystals are converted into hollow nanocages with the cavity size and shell thickness of ${\approx}150$ and $15~\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. In contrast, negligible morphology change of $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ is observed. The high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmis­ sion electron microscopy (HAADF-STEM) image further dem­ onstrates the hollow nanocages are assembled on top–down surfaces of solid MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . Corresponding elemental map­ ping images (Figure 1e) and line scan spectrum (Figure S6a,b, Supporting Information) show that the middle Ti rich nanocake is sandwiched by Co/Fe rich nanocages. The average atomic ratio of ${\mathrm{Co}}/{\mathrm{Fe}}$ is estimated to be 1.4 from the spot scanning spectrum (Figure S6c, Supporting Information), close to the theoretical value (1.5) of CoFe PBA.[36] The Ti/Co/Fe ratio was determined to be $5.2/1/0.6$ by inductive coupled plasma emis­ sion spectrometer (ICP, Table S1, Supporting Information), indicative of a MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ /CoFe PBA mass ratio of $6.6/1$ .
The crystal structure of MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA was inves­ tigated by XRD. As shown in Figure  1f, the diffraction peaks of $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ are observed, but not ZIF-67. Besides, a new group of peaks attributed to CoFe PBA are observed, indicating the conversion of ZIF-67 into CoFe PBA. The Fourier trans­ form infrared (FTIR) spectrum of MIL-125- $\mathrm{\DeltaNH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA is presented in Figure  1g with CoFe PBA and $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ for comparison. The CoFe PBA with a hollow structure was syn­ thesized by using pre-synthesized ZIF-67 nanocubes as precur­ sors followed by the same ion exchange process [Figures S7 and S8, (Supporting Information) see details in Experimental Section].[37] The typical groups of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ , $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C-O}}$ , and $\mathrm{Ti-O}$ bands at 1631, 1250, and $767~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ and CoFe PBA (stretching vibration of bridging cyano group at $2081~\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ ) are simultaneously detected.[38,39] The $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , CoFe PBA and MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA are shown in Figure S9 (Supporting Information). The Brunauer– Emmett–Teller (BET) specific surface areas and total pore volume are calculated to be 1345, 415, $202~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and 0.724, 0.364, $0.540\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , MIL-125- $\left.\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}@\mathrm{CoFe}\right.$ PBA, and CoFe PBA, respectively (Table S2, Supporting Infor­ mation). Collectively, a sandwich-like MOF-on-MOF heterostructure has been synthesized with CoFe PBA hollow nanocages adhered on the top–down surfaces of $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ nanocakes via an innovative MOF-on-MOF to MOF-on-MOF transformation strategy. Moreover, both the complex structure and composition are different from reported MOF-on-MOF hybrids.[26,40,41]
The optical property of $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{CoFt}$ PBA was explored by UV–vis diffuse reflectance spectra (DRS). For comparison, CoFe PBA hollow cubes, $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , and physically mixed MIL- $125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ CoFe PBA composites were also investigated. As shown in Figure 2a, $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ exhibits an absorption edge at $\approx463~\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to a bandgap $(E_{\mathrm{g}})$ of $2.68~\mathrm{eV}$ (Figure  2b). The light absorption of MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA $(E_{\mathrm{g}}=2.59~\mathrm{eV})$ in the Vis–NIR light region is significantly enhanced compared with $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , which may be ascribed to the integration of CoFe PBA with darker color and narrowed bandgap $(E_{\mathrm{g}}=1.92~\mathrm{eV})$ . The peak at ${\approx}610$ nm may be originated from the metal to metal $(\mathrm{Ti{-}O{-}C o})$ charge transfer between MIL- ${\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and CoFe PBA.[42] Fur­ thermore, compared to physical mixture of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/$ CoFe PBA, MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA heterostructure exhibits stronger light absorption capacity, suggesting the importance of heterojunctions in the sandwich-like structure of MIL$125\ –\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA.
The flat band potential $(E_{\mathrm{fb}})$ of CoFe PBA, $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA was further determined by Mott–Schottky plots (Figure  2c). The Mott–Schottky curves of CoFe PBA, $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{CoFe}$ PBA exhibit positive slopes, indicative of their n-type semiconductor characteristics. The $\ensuremath{E_{\mathrm{fb}}}$ of $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , CoFe PBA, and MIL$125\substack{-\mathrm{NH}_{2}@}$ CoFe PBA was measured to be −0.72, $-1.04$ , and $-0.82\ \mathrm{~eV}$ versus normal hydrogen electrode (NHE), respec­ tively, according to the equation: $E_{\mathrm{NHE}}=E_{\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}}-E^{\theta}+0.059~\mathrm{pH}$ $\big(E_{\mathrm{~\tiny~(Ag/AgCl,~pH~7)~}}^{\theta}=0.197\mathrm{~V}\big)$ .[43] In general, the bottom of CB in n-type semiconductors was ${\approx}0.1\mathrm{V}$ lower than the $\ensuremath{E_{\mathrm{fb}}}$ ,[44] the CB values of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , CoFe PBA, and $\mathrm{MIL-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{CoFe}$ PBA were thus calculated to be $-0.62,\ -0.94$ and $-0.72\ \mathrm{~eV}$ versus NHE. After calculation, the valance band (VB) values of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , CoFe PBA, and MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA are 2.06, 0.98, and $1.87\mathrm{eV},$ , respectively. The detailed band structures of MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , CoFe PBA, and MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA are illustrated in Figure 2d, showing a well-matched and staggered band alignment between CoFe PBA and MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ .
In order to investigate the photocarrier separation and transfer manners of all samples, steady-state photolumines­ cence (PL) spectroscopy, time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) spectra, photocurrent tests, and electrochemical imped­ ance spectroscopy (EIS) were conducted. Among all samples, MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA exhibits the weakest PL emission peak intensity (Figure  2e), indicating the lowest electron-hole recombination efficiency. The photogenerated charge car­ rier transfer dynamics were further surveyed by TRPL spectra (Figure  2f). The average PL lifetime $(\tau_{\mathrm{avg}})$ of MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA was calculated to be 9.59  ns, longer than MIL${125.}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (5.67  ns), CoFe PBA (7.66  ns) and $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/$ CoFe PBA $(6.78\ \mathrm{ns})$ , suggesting the longer-lived charge pairs in $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{CoFe}$ PBA. The PL and TRPL results indi­ cate the crucial role of heterojunction in facilitating the photo­ carrier migration, which is further evidenced by the highest current density and lowest electron transfer resistance of MIL$125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA in the photocurrent curves (Figure  2g) and Nyquist plots (Figure 2h), respectively.
To investigate the interfacial interaction between MIL${125.}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and CoFe PBA in $\mathrm{MIL-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{CoFe}$ PBA, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) analysis was conducted. The XPS survey spectrum (Figure S10a, Supporting Information) of MIL-125- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA shows the co-existence of Ti, Co, Fe, C, N, and O elements. High-resolution XPS spectra of Ti 2p, Co 2p, O 1s, and Fe 2p are displayed in Figure 3a–c and Figure S10 (Supporting Information). The Ti $2\mathrm{p}$ spectrum of MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (Figure 3a) shows two peaks of Ti $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ and $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ at 464.8 and $459.1\mathrm{eV},$ respectively, in accordance with the literature report.[13] Compared with $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , the binding energies of Ti $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ and Ti $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ peaks of MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA present a negative shift of $\approx0.8{\mathrm{~eV.}}$ For $\mathrm{Co}\ 2\mathrm{p}$ spectrum of CoFe PBA (Figure 3b), the peaks at 780.5 and $795.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to the $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ states of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ . In addition, two peaks at 783.1 and $796.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ attributed to the $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ states of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ are deconvoluted, with two satellite peaks at 787.9 and 804.1 eV.[45,46] After forming the heterojunctions, the binding energies of Co
2p peaks of MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA are $\approx1.3\ \mathrm{eV}$ higher than CoFe PBA, in contrast to the negative shift of Ti 2p.
The O 1s spectra of samples are shown in Figure 3c. For MIL${125.}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , the O 1s spectrum can be can be fitted into two peaks at 532.0 and $530.4\ \mathrm{eV},$ attributed to $0-\mathrm{C=}0$ and $\mathrm{Ti-O}$ bond.[47] For CoFe PBA, the peaks centered at 531.2 and $529.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to the $-\mathrm{OH}$ group and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co-O}$ bond, respectively.[36] In the spectrum of MIL-125- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA, the peaks at 531.7 and $532~\mathrm{eV}$ are assigned to $-\mathrm{OH}$ and $0-\mathrm{C=}0$ groups. Another peak at $529.8\mathrm{eV},$ located between $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Co-O}$ bond of CoFe PBA and Ti−O bond of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , belongs to Ti−O−Co bond.[32,48] According to literature,[49–51] unsaturated Co sites can be easily formed for further modification in CoFe PBA, while the Fe ion is strongly coordinated to six C-bonded cyanides and thus stable. Consequently, Ti−O−Co rather than Ti−O−Fe bond is formed at the interface, as evidenced by the negligible binding energy change of Fe 2p in XPS spectrum MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathsf{C o F e}$ PBA when compared to CoFe PBA (Figure S10b, Supporting Information).
The higher shift degree of Ti, O, and Co elements in the XPS spectra of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{Col}$ Fe PBA heterojunction than physically mixed MIL-125- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}/$ CoFe PBA indicates the forma­ tion of more intimate interface with higher amount of chem­ ical bonds (Ti−O−Co) between $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and CoFe PBA in MIL-125-NH2@CoFe PBA.[52,53] Based on the XPS observations, it is inferred that $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@(\$ CoFe  PBA  heterostructure exhibits chemical interaction with $\mathrm{Ti-O-Co}$ bond formation at the interface via MOF-on-MOF hybridization, facilitating the electron transfer from Ti in $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ to Co in CoFe PBA. The strong chemical interaction and charge redistribution may induce the formation of internal electric field within CoFe PBA/ $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ heterojunction, which can provide potent driving force for photogenerated charge migration.[17]
To further validate the formation of Ti−O−Co bond, the pre­ cise structure of MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA was analyzed by synchrotron X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS). Figure 3d,e shows the Fourier transformed (FT) K-space extended XAFS (EXAFS) spectrums of MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA at Ti and Co K-edge with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and CoO as reference samples. For the Ti K-edge spectrum (Figure 3d), the peak at $\mathrm{\simeq}1.5\mathrm{\stackrel{\scriptscriptstyle~A}{A}}$ is assigned to Ti−O pair, similar to the Ti−O bond in $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . Another peak at ${\approx}3.6\mathrm{~\AA~}$ is found in MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA, but not in $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , which could be assigned to $\mathrm{Ti-O-Co}$ bond. In the Co K-edge spectrum (Figure  3e), in addition to the $\mathrm{Co-O/N}$ and Co-Co bonds, the peak at ${\approx}3.6$ Å may also be attributed to the Ti−O−Co bond. Moreover, wavelet transform was employed to visualize the coordination environment of the Ti and Co atoms (Figure 3f–i), further suggesting the existence of Ti−O−Co bond in M $_{\mathrm{L}-125-N\mathrm{H}_{2}@\mathrm{CoF}\epsilon}$ e PBA heterostructure, in agreement with the XPS results.
As another important parameter for determining the elec­ tron transfer within heterostructure, the work function $(\Phi)$ of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , CoFe PBA, and $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{Co}$ Fe PBA was measured by ultraviolet photo-electron spectroscopy (UPS) studies (Figure 4a), with a monochromatic He light source $(21.22~\mathrm{eV})$ .[30] The VB top energy (vs vacuum) of MIL${125.}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ was calculated as $-(21.22-16.98)-2.22~=~-6.46\mathrm{eV}.$ Thus, the  valance  band  potential $\left(E_{\mathrm{v}}\right)$ of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ was calculated as: $(-6.46)-4.44\ =\ 2.02\mathrm{V}$ versus normal hydrogen electrode (NHE). Because the $E_{\mathrm{g}}$ of $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ is $2.68\mathrm{eV},$ the conduction band potential $\left(E_{\mathrm{c}}\right)$ versus NHE was calculated by $E_{\mathrm{v}}-E_{\mathrm{g}}=-0.66\mathrm{V}.$ Meanwhile, the $E_{\mathrm{v}}$ versus NHE of CoFe PBA and MIL-125- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA was determined to be 0.9 and 1.71 V, respectively. The $E_{\mathrm{c}}$ versus NHE of CoFe PBA and MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA was determined to be $-1.02$ and $-0.88\mathrm{V},$ respectively, close to the results of Mott–Schottky plots. In addition, the $\Phi$ values of $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and CoFe PBA were calculated as: $21.22{-}16.98\ =\ 4.24\mathrm{eV}$ and $21.22\mathrm{-}17.5=3.72\mathrm{eV}$ (vs vacuum), respectively. Furthermore, Fermi level $\left(E_{\mathrm{f}}\right)$ of these two samples was calculated on the basis of the following Equation 1:
$$
E_{\mathrm{f}}=V_{\mathrm{ac}}-\Phi
$$
where $V_{\mathrm{ac}}$ is the vacuum level (assumed as $0~\mathrm{eV}$ ).[52] Thus, $E_{\mathrm{f}}$ values of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and CoFe PBA were determined to be $-4.24$ and $-3.72\mathrm{eV}.$ respectively.
The photoirradiated Kelvin probe force microscopy (KPFM) was conducted to further investigate the spatial charge sepa­ ration manners in $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{CoF}\epsilon$ PBA heterojunction. The topographic image (Figure 4b) viewed parallel to the nano­ cake top/bottom surface shows the sandwich-like morphology of MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA heterostructure, in agreement with the SEM and TEM observations. In an illustrative line scan crossing the interface of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and CoFe PBA (Figure 4c), the surface potential in CoFe PBA region is $\mathrm{\approx}15~\mathrm{mV}$ higher than MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ region in the dark, demonstrating the free electron flow direction from CoFe PBA to $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . This phenomenon is consistent with the XPS results, further revealing the formation of internal electric field with the direc­ tion pointing from $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ to CoFe PBA. Upon light irra­ diation, the surface potential in CoFe PBA region was obviously decreased, while the surface potential in $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ region was raised. This observation indicates a S-scheme migration of photogenerated electrons from $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ to CoFe PBA, which is also supported by the surface potential change of CoFe PBA with/without light irradiation on a particle viewed vertical to the nanocake top/bottom surface (Figure S11a,b, Supporting Information). In addition, a gold deposition experiment was performed, involving the reduction of ${\mathrm{Au}}^{3+}$ from $\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}$ to ${\mathrm{Au}}^{0}$ by photogenerated electrons.[54] The selective deposition of Au nanoparticles was observed in the region of CoFe PBA rather than $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (Figure S12, Supporting Information). This observation indicates the photoelectron accumulation on CoFe PBA, causing reduction and growth of Au nanoparticles, con­ sistent with the proposed S-scheme mechanism.
Density functional theory (DFT) calculations were performed to further understand the interfacial interaction and electronic properties of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{Cc}$ Fe PBA. The surface energy $\left(E_{\mathrm{s}}\right)$ of CoFe PBA (001), (110), and (111) facets was obtained by
$$
E_{\mathrm{s}}=\left(E_{\mathrm{tot}}-n_{\mathrm{i}}\sum_{\mathrm{i}}\mu_{\mathrm{i}}\right)/2\mathrm{A}\left(\mathrm{i}=\mathrm{Co},\mathrm{Fe},\mathrm{N},\mathrm{C},\mathrm{and}\mathrm{K}\right)
$$
where $E_{\mathrm{tot}}$ is the total energy of a selected CoFe PBA surface, $n$ and $\mu$ represents the number and chemical potential of consid­ ered atoms, respectively, and $A$ is the surface area. The metal and CN terminal (Figure S13, Supporting Information) were considered for each facet to identify the most stable one of CoFe PBA. As shown in Table S3 (Supporting Information), the (100) facet with the metal terminal is most likely to be exposed due to its lowest energy $(8.05\ \mathrm{J}\ \mathrm{m}^{-2})$ . Besides, the CoFe PBA is adhered on the top–down surfaces of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , which are assigned to (001) facets according to literatures.[26–28] Based on this model, the optimized MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA het­ erostructure with a calculated formation energy of $-1.42\ \mathrm{eV}$ is obtained, as illustrated in Figure 4d.
At the interface of optimized heterostructure, the surface Co of CoFe PBA is bonded with the surface O of MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (CoO bond length of 2.85 Å), in accordance with the forma­ tion of $_{\mathrm{Ti-O-Co}}$ bond observed by XPS and EXAFS. In the top view of the interlayer, each Co locates above the center of the six-membered $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{Ti-O-C}}$ ring of the $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ surface. The charge density difference (Figure 4e) shows that electrons are mainly accumulated around the O atoms but depleted from the Co atoms in the $_{\mathrm{Ti-O-Co}}$ bridges, suggesting the inter­ layer electron transfer from CoFe PBA to $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . The interfacial electron transfer leads to a built-in electric field pointing from CoFe PBA to $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , in accordance with the XPS and UPS observations. In addition, the partial density of states of MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA heterostructure was also calculated, as shown in Figure 4f. The bands below Fermi Level are mainly contributed by CoFe PBA, while those above Fermi Level are predominately generated from $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ in the heterostructure, further supporting the electron transfer from CoFe PBA to $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ at the heterojunction.
From the experimental and calculation results, a charge transfer mechanism of S-scheme junction between MIL${125.}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and CoFe PBA in the heterostructure is depicted in Figure  4g–i. MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ exhibits lower band positions and Fermi level in comparison with CoFe PBA (Figure $^{4\mathrm{g})}$ . Upon the contact between MIL- ${\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and CoFe PBA in the het­ erostructure stabilized by $_{\mathrm{Ti-O-Co}}$ bonds (Figure  4h), the Fermi levels are aligned and there exists a build-in electric field pointing from CoFe PBA to $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , resulting downward and upward band bending of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and CoFe PBA, respectively. Under irradiation (Figure 4i), the electrons in MIL$125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and CoFe PBA are photoexcited from their VB to CB though a ligand-to-metal charge transfer (LMCT) process.[55,56] Driven by the build-in electric field, band bending, and Cou­ lomb force, the photogenerated electrons in CB of MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ are transferred to the VB of CoFe PBA via the $_{\mathrm{Ti-O-Co}}$ chan­ nels for consumption of the photogenerated holes. As a result, the photogenerated holes and electrons are left in the VB of $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ and CB of CoFe PBA, respectively, thus the redox capability is maximumly preserved. Notably, even great advances have been achieved in the field of MOF-on-MOF hybrids, a S-scheme heterojunction with interfacial chemical bonds has been rarely reported. In addition, the hollow and sandwich-like structure can promote the surface accessibility of photocatalysts, which is also beneficial for enhancing the photo­catalytic performance.[57]
The photocatalytic activity of MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA heter­ ostructure was evaluated through tetracycline degradation under visible light $(\lambda\ge420~\mathrm{nm})$ irradiation with $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , CoFe PBA, and MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}/$ CoFe PBA as comparison. As shown in Figure 5a, CoFe PBA showed a higher adsorption ratio of $46.8\%$ than $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(11.0\%)$ , $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\mathrm{CoF}\epsilon$ e PBA $(16.9\%)$ , and MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA $(29.4\%)$ after $40~\mathrm{min}$ adsorption in the dark. When the photocatalytic reaction was switched on by visible light irradiation, MIL-125-NH2@CoFe PBA exhibits a remove rate of $86.9\%$ after $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ , higher than that of CoFe PBA $(58.9\%)$ , $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(24.3\%)$ and $\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}/\mathrm{CoF}\epsilon$ e PBA $(39.1\%)$ . Furthermore, the kinetic curve of tetracycline degrada­ tion was simulated by the equation $-\mathrm{In}(\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{C}_{0})=k_{\mathrm{t}},$ where $\mathbf{\xi}_{t}$ is the photocatalytic reaction time and $\mathrm{\Deltak}$ is the rate constant.[58] The tetracycline degradation behaviors of these four samples con­ form to pseudo first order kinetics (Figure  5b). The $\mathrm{\Deltak}$ value of
MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA $(0.014\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , Figure  5c) is 14, 7, and 4.7 times higher than that of $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(0.001\mathrm{min^{-1}})$ , CoFe PBA $(0.002\ \mathrm{min^{-1}})$ and MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}/$ CoFe PBA $(0.003\ \mathrm{min^{-1}})$ , implying the fastest reaction kinetic. Notably, the reaction kinetic of MIL-125- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathsf{C o F e}$ PBA for photodegradation of tetracycline also exceeds most reported MOF-based photocatalysts and even other non-MOF heterostructures (Table S4, Supporting Informa­ tion), further manifesting the superior photocatalytic activity.
To further demonstrate the crucial role of ion change treat­ ment in our design, the optical property and photocatalytic per­ formance of MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67$ heterostructure were meas­ ured for comparison. As shown in Figures S14 and S15 (Sup­ porting Information), $M\mathrm{IL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}67$ exhibits type I heterojunction with obviously inferior tetracycline degradation efficacy (remove rate of $40.6\%$ ) than MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA $(86.9\%)$ . Furthermore, the photocatalytic degradation studies of other typical pollutants including organic dye (rhodamine B, RhB) and phenolic compound (4-Nitrophenol, 4-NP) have been conducted. The results show that after $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ reaction, the removal rates of RhB and 4-NP reach $95.4\%$ and $80.5\%$ , respectively (Figure S16, Supporting Information), demonstrating the per­ formance universality of designed S-type MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA heterojunction.
In addition, the electron spin resonance (ESR) tests were used to identify the main reactive species during the photocat­ alytic tetracycline degradation. As shown in Figure  5d,e, EPR signals corresponding to · ${\mathrm{O}}_{2}^{-}$ and ·OH radicals were detected in all samples, which are regarded as strongly oxidative species for organic pollutants degradation.[59,60] Here, under the attack of the ${\mathrm{O}}_{2}^{-}$ and ·OH radicals, the tetracycline may be oxidized into smaller molecule intermediates and subsequently mineral­ ized into $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .[61,62] Compared to $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ , CoFe PBA and MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ /CoFe PBA, the signal intensities of both · ${\mathrm{O}}_{2}^{-}$ and ⋅OH radicals for $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{CoFe}$ PBA are obviously stronger, in accordance with the higher tetracycline degradation efficiency. Moreover, $\mathrm{\backslashIIL-125{\cdot}N H_{2}@C o F e}$ PBA also demonstrates a robust photocatalytic activity with only $\approx4\%$ decay of degradation efficiency after five cycles (Figure 5f). The TEM image (Figure S17, Supporting Information) of MIL$125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{CoFe}$ PBA after use also shows well-reserved mor­ phology, further indicating its good stability.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we have constructed a S-scheme $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@$ CoFe PBA heterojunction with excellent photocatalytic per­ formance for degradation of various organic pollutants. Dif­ ferent from traditional MOF/non-MOF couples, the innovative MOF-on-MOF design in S-scheme heterojunction integrates the merits of two different semiconducting MOF units into one photocatalyst by creating an intimate interface bridged by well-defined chemical bonds. The S-scheme charge transfer mechanism and the formation of interfacial TiOCo bridges in MIL-125- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ @CoFe PBA heterojunction are supported by both experimental and theoretical studies. Our work expands the structures of MOF-based heterojunctions and applications of MOF-on-MOF hybrids, providing new insights into the design of MOF-based functional materials for photocatalysis.
## 4. Experimental Section
Materials: Titanium isopropoxide (TPOT, $97\%$ Aldrich), cobalt nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ from Sinopharm Chemical Regent Co., Ltd, potassium hexacyanoferrate (III) $(\mathsf{K}_{3}\mathsf{F e}(\mathsf{C N})_{6}$ , $99\%$ , Adamas-beta), 2-aminoterephthalate ( $N{\mathsf{H}}_{2}$ -BDC, $99\%$ , Aldrich), 2-methylimidazole (2-MeIM, Aldrich), N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, $99\%$ , Greagent), methanol (AR, $99\%$ , Adamas-beta), ethanol (AR, $99\%$ , Adamas-beta) and tetracycline (BR, $90\%+,$ Adamas-beta) were used as obtained. All the aqueous solutions referred were prepared using deionized water.
Fabrication of $M I L-725-N H_{2}$ : $M\L_{1}\L_{L}\L_{-}\L_{2}\Sigma_{-}\L_{N}\L_{H_{2}}$ was prepared according to a previous report.[35] TPOT $(0.54~\mathsf{m L})$ and $N\mathsf{H}_{2}$ -BDC $_{(0.66~\mathrm{g})}$ were dissolved in $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of mixed solution $(V_{\mathsf{D M F}}/V_{\mathsf{m e t h a n o l}}=7;\mathsf{l})$ ) under stirring for 5 min. Afterward, the mixture solution was heated at $\mathsf{l}50^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $75\dot{\mathsf{h}}$ under a static condition in a $50~\mathsf{m L}$ Teflon-line autoclave. After cooling to room temperature, the yellow powder was collected and subsequently dispersed in $30~\mathsf{m L}$ of methanol to make a stock solution for further use. Fabrication of $M I L-125.N H_{2}@Z I F.G7$ Heterostructures: The synthesis was carried out using our previously reported route with slight modification.[26] Specifically, $0.4~\mathrm{mL}$ of $M L-725\cdot N H_{2}$ methanol solution, $5~\mathsf{m L}$ of $80~\mathsf{m m}$ 2-MeIM solution, and $3m L$ of $25\mathrm{\mw\Co(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ solution were mixed together. After reacting for $2h$ , the final products were obtained by centrifugation and aid-dried at $30^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ overnight.
Fabrication of $M I L-725-N H_{2}@C o F e$ PBA Heterostructures: MIL${125.111}_{2}@2{1}\mathsf{F}{-}67$ ( $\left[70\ m g\right)$ was dispersed into $70~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol through ultrasonication for $75\mathrm{\min}$ . Then, $7.5~\mathsf{m g}$ of $k_{3}[\mathsf{F e}(\mathsf{C N})_{6}]$ was dissolved in $3.5~\mathsf{m L}$ of $H_{2}O$ to form a transparent solution, which was injected into the $M!L-725-N\mapsto2C-67$ solution rapidly with continuous stirring at room temperature for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ . Finally, the MIL-125-NH2@CoFe PBA precipitate was collected using centrifugation, washed three times with ethanol, and dried at $60^{\circ}C$ .
Fabrication of ZIF-67: In a typical synthesis,[37] $58.3~\mathrm{~mg~}$ of $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ was dissolved in $2m L$ of CTAB aqueous solution $(7.5\mathrm{~g~L^{-1})}$ . The solution was rapidly poured into $75m L$ of aqueous solution containing $908m g$ of 2-MeIM. After stirring at room temperature for 20 min, the product was obtained by centrifugation and washed by ethanol for five times.
Fabrication of CoFe PBA: For preparation of CoFe PBA,[37] $22~{\mathsf{m g}}$ of ZIF-67 was dispersed into $75m L$ of ethanol through ultrasonication for 15  min, followed by adding $75m L$ of $k_{3}[\mathsf{F e}(\mathsf{C N})_{6}]$ aqueous solution $(3.3\mathrm{~g~L^{-1}})$ with stirring at room temperature for 16  h. CoFe PBA were then collected by centrifugation, washed with ethanol for three times.
Photodeposition of Au: MIL-125-NH2@CoFe PBA powder $(70~\mathsf{m g})$ was dispersed in $20~\mathsf{m L}$ of $H A u C l_{4}$ solution $(40\ m g\ L^{-1})$ . The mixture was irradiated under a 300  W Xe lamp equipping with stirring for $2h$ . The products were then washed by water, centrifuged, and dried at $50~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ overnight for further use.
Material Characterization: The morphology of the samples was characterized by field-emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM, HITACHI-S4800) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEOL 2100F). The element analysis was conducted using a field-emission scanning electron microscope (FIB, Helios G4 UX, FEI Inc. USA) with energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX, X-Max 150T, Oxford, UK) attachment. X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were acquired on Bruker D8 advanced X-ray diffractometer with $\mathsf{C u}\ \mathsf{K}\alpha$ radiation ( $(\lambda=$ $0.754\:\:\:\mathsf{n m}_{,}^{\prime}$ . X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra were collected by a PHI Quantera II ESCA System with Al $\mathsf{K}\alpha$ radiation at $\mathsf{l}486.8\ \mathsf{V}.$ The extended $\mathsf{X}$ -ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) was measured by the synchrotron radiation source and analyzed using Athena and Artemis softwares.[63] The energy calibration of the sample was conducted through a standard $\mathsf{T i O}_{2}$ and CoO, which as a reference was simultaneously measured. Fourier Transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FTIR) spectra were obtained via a Thermal-Nicolet iS50 spectrometer between 4000 and $500~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ . $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms were measured by using Micromeritics ASAP-2460 at $-796^{\circ}C$ . Diffuse reflectance UV–vis absorption spectroscopy was recorded in the spectral region of $200{-}800~\mathsf{n m}$ on a UV–vis spectrophotometer (Lambda950). The concentration of organic pollutants was measured by a BioTek Synergy
H1 Hybrid Multi-Mode Monochromator. Ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) studies were conducted by using an ESCALAB $250~\times1$ analyzer with He $(27.22~\mathsf{e V})$ as monochromatic light source. The photo-irradiated Kelvin probe force microscopy (KPFM) (Bruker Dimension ICON) was used to test the surface potential of the samples. Stead-state photoluminescence (PL) emission spectra were examined using an Edinburgh FS5 spectrofluorometer in the range of $380{\-}700\ \mathsf{n m}$ . Time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) spectra were collected on a FSL980 transient fluorescence spectrometer. Electron spin resonance (ESR) spectra were recorded on a Bruker A300 ESR spectrometer.
Photocatalytic Activity Measurements: Photocatalysts $(0.2~\mathrm{~g~}\:\:\mathsf{L}^{-1})$ were dispersed in $20\ m L\ 50\ m g\ \mathsf{L}^{-1}$ tetracycline/RhB/4-NP solution. The suspension was first stirred in dark for 40  min to reach the adsorption-desorption equilibrium. Subsequently, the photocatalytic reaction was initiated by the irradiation of a 300  W xenon lamp (PLSSXE300D/300DUV, Beijing Perfectlight) equipped with a thermostatic water bath at $25^{\circ}C$ and a $420\ \mathsf{n m}$ cutoff filter as visible light source in a dark container. $0.2~\mathsf{m L}$ of reaction solution was collected at regular time intervals $(20\mathrm{\min})$ and filtrated with $0.45~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ filters to remove the photocatalysts. The concentration of tetracycline was determined with a UV spectrophotometer at $360~\mathsf{n m}$ .[64] The degradation rate of tetracycline was evaluated by the Equation 3:[61]
$C_{0}$ : the initial concentration of tetracycline
Ct: the instantaneous concentration of tetracycline after a certain irradiation time (t)
Photoelectrochemical Measurements: The photoelectrochemical measurements were performed on a CHI 760E electrochemistry workstation (ChenHua Instruments Co., China) using $0.7~\mathsf{m}$ ${\mathsf{N a}}_{2}{\mathsf{S O}}_{4}$ solution $\left(\mathsf{p}\mathsf{H}\ 6.8\right)$ as electrolyte. The platinum wire, $\sf A g/A g C l$ , and Indium-doped tin oxide (ITO) modified with photocatalysts were used as counter, reference, and working electrodes, respectively. The working electrode was prepared as follows: $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{70}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{m}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{g}}$ of catalysts was first mixed with $40~\upmu\up L$ of $10\%$ Nafion solution to produce a slurry. Afterward, the slurry was pipetted onto a piece of ITO glass (effective area: $\mathsf{1}(\mathsf{m}^{2})$ and then dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . The photocurrent response was recorded using a 300 W xenon lamp (PLS-SXE300D/300DUV, Beijing Perfectlight) equipped with a $420~\mathsf{n m}$ cutoff filter. The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was measured at the open circuit potential with a frequency range from $0.01~\mathsf{H z}$ to $7000k H z$ under irradiation. In order to determine the flat-band potential, Mott–Schottky (M–S) plots were collected at a fixed frequency of ${\mathsf{1000}}\mathsf{H z}$ .
Computational Details: Density functional theory (DFT) calculations were implemented in the Vienna ab-initio simulation package (VASP),[65,66] within the Perdew–Burke–Emzerhof functional of generalized gradient approximation (GGA-PBE) and the projector augmented wave method (PAW) method.[67,68] The kinetic cut-off energy was $400~\mathrm{eV},$ while the $3\times3\times1$ Monkhorst k-mesh was adopted due to the large lattice parameters of the heterostructure. A vacuum layer of $>$ $20\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ is constructed to screen the interactions from the periodic adjacent layers along the $z$ direction. DFT-D3 correction is considered for van der Waals interaction.[69] The criterions for energy and force on each atom were set to be $\mathsf{10^{-3}~e V}$ and $0.07\ \mathrm{eV}\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}}^{-1}$ , respectively. The position of the bottom atomic layer of MIL- $725\cdot N H_{2}$ is fixed to simulate its surface after forming the heterostructure. The formation energy is calculated by $E_{\mathsf{f}}=$ $\begin{array}{r}{E_{\mathrm{tot}}{-}E_{\mathrm{MlL}}{-}E_{\mathsf{P B A}},}\end{array}$ , where $E_{\mathrm{tot}},$ $E_{\mathrm{ML}},$ and $\boldsymbol{E}_{\mathsf{P B A}}$ represent the total energy of the MIL-125- $\cdot N H_{2}@$ CoFe PBA, $M L-725-N H_{2}$ , and CoFe PBA layers, respectively.
